WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.493 --> 00:00:03.539
Before we introduce statistical measures,

2
00:00:03.619 --> 00:00:06.885
we need a short detour. We've seen that a
sample

3
00:00:06.965 --> 00:00:10.559
may not accurately represent the target
population.

4
00:00:10.639 --> 00:00:13.795
That's why we need to study uncertainty.

5
00:00:13.875 --> 00:00:17.689
And the language we use for that is
probability.

6
00:00:17.769 --> 00:00:20.785
For example, when I roll a die, I know

7
00:00:20.877 --> 00:00:24.340
that each number from 1 to 6 has the same
chance.

8
00:00:24.420 --> 00:00:26.626
But I can't predict

9
00:00:26.706 --> 00:00:29.655
exactly which number will come up next.

10
00:00:29.735 --> 00:00:33.999
In theory, if I knew with infinite precision
the die's speed

11
00:00:34.079 --> 00:00:38.000
as I threw it, its rotation, the wind speed
and every

12
00:00:38.080 --> 00:00:42.115
other variable affecting it, I could

13
00:00:42.195 --> 00:00:47.281
reconstruct its path and predict the next roll
with certainty.

14
00:00:47.373 --> 00:00:49.900
In practice, though, that's impossible:

15
00:00:49.980 --> 00:00:52.900
there is too much uncertainty in what I know.

16
00:00:52.980 --> 00:00:55.833
So we treat the outcome as random and use
probability.

17
00:00:56.333 --> 00:00:58.950
Let's define a few terms. An 'outcome'

18
00:00:59.030 --> 00:01:02.033
is what we get from a single trial.

19
00:01:02.113 --> 00:01:05.225
If I roll the die once, the outcome

20
00:01:05.305 --> 00:01:08.693
can be any number from 1 to 6.

21
00:01:08.773 --> 00:01:12.931
Depending on that outcome, certain conditions
may or may not hold.

22
00:01:13.011 --> 00:01:16.369
For example, consider 'rolling an even
number'.

23
00:01:16.525 --> 00:01:20.691
That condition is met if I roll 2, 4 or 6.

24
00:01:20.771 --> 00:01:22.516
This is what we call an event.

25
00:01:22.596 --> 00:01:24.098
An event can also be very simple:

26
00:01:24.178 --> 00:01:26.531
'I roll a 1', for instance.

27
00:01:26.611 --> 00:01:29.225
We could define many different events

28
00:01:29.305 --> 00:01:32.166
in this setting. The set of all possible
outcomes

29
00:01:32.246 --> 00:01:35.593
is called the sample space, sometimes written
as omega.

30
00:01:36.013 --> 00:01:39.824
To understand probability,

31
00:01:39.904 --> 00:01:44.075
let's roll this die over and over again.

32
00:01:44.155 --> 00:01:48.206
Here, each time I roll it,

33
00:01:48.286 --> 00:01:53.235
I add a dot above the corresponding number.

34
00:01:53.315 --> 00:01:57.745
For example, I roll a 4 and put a dot in
column 4.

35
00:01:57.837 --> 00:02:01.803
I roll a 6 and put a dot in column 6, and so
on.

36
00:02:02.286 --> 00:02:06.144
With only a few rolls, randomness means

37
00:02:06.224 --> 00:02:10.304
there is no reason for every number to appear
equally often.

38
00:02:10.384 --> 00:02:13.022
Here, for example, I've rolled

39
00:02:13.102 --> 00:02:17.706
more sixes and fewer threes.

40
00:02:17.786 --> 00:02:19.900
If I'd been playing Monopoly, I'd have been
lucky!

41
00:02:19.980 --> 00:02:23.474
But that won't necessarily happen every time.

42
00:02:23.566 --> 00:02:27.005
For example, let's try another series,

43
00:02:27.085 --> 00:02:30.697
another 18 rolls. This time,

44
00:02:30.777 --> 00:02:34.735
I've rolled seven fours, so I've been a little
less lucky.

45
00:02:34.815 --> 00:02:37.966
We can run these simulations again and again.

46
00:02:38.046 --> 00:02:42.465
As we roll the die more and more times,

47
00:02:42.545 --> 00:02:47.218
the proportions tend towards the theoretical
probabilities.

48
00:02:48.528 --> 00:02:52.515
After these 30 rolls, I got a 3 on 23% of
rolls,

49
00:02:52.595 --> 00:02:58.583
but a 6 on only 6.7% of rolls.

50
00:02:58.663 --> 00:03:00.399
So I wasn't particularly lucky this time.

51
00:03:00.479 --> 00:03:03.153
If I now start rolling

52
00:03:03.233 --> 00:03:07.220
a very large number of times, we gradually
approach a situation

53
00:03:07.300 --> 00:03:10.804
with roughly equal proportions of ones, twos
and every other number.

54
00:03:11.280 --> 00:03:14.681
Random fluctuations

55
00:03:14.761 --> 00:03:19.195
have a large relative effect when there are
few rolls,

56
00:03:19.275 --> 00:03:23.219
but that effect decreases as the number of
trials grows.

57
00:03:23.299 --> 00:03:26.374
In the limit, as the number of trials tends to
infinity,

58
00:03:26.454 --> 00:03:30.181
each number has the same limiting proportion.

59
00:03:30.261 --> 00:03:32.980
That gives us an intuitive way to understand
probability.

60
00:03:33.072 --> 00:03:36.637
So there is a difference between the observed
frequency,

61
00:03:36.717 --> 00:03:40.566
which comes from our trials, and probability:

62
00:03:40.646 --> 00:03:44.633
the theoretical chance of each value under the
model.

63
00:03:45.120 --> 00:03:48.156
A few basic rules apply

64
00:03:48.236 --> 00:03:51.273
to probabilities. Let's look at them now.

65
00:03:51.353 --> 00:03:54.735
First, a probability is always between 0 and
1.

66
00:03:54.815 --> 00:03:59.236
Suppose I have a 70% chance of getting a
particular value.

67
00:03:59.316 --> 00:04:03.391
That corresponds to 0.7. A probability cannot
be below

68
00:04:03.471 --> 00:04:07.684
0 or above 1; a certain event has probability
1.

69
00:04:09.312 --> 00:04:12.992
Next, let's look at the sum of probabilities.

70
00:04:13.072 --> 00:04:17.105
Here we'll use an unfair die:

71
00:04:17.185 --> 00:04:21.395
its faces don't all have the same chance of
coming up.

72
00:04:21.475 --> 00:04:23.627
For example, I have a one-in-twelve chance

73
00:04:23.707 --> 00:04:26.976
of rolling a 1, but a one-in-six chance of
rolling a 3.

74
00:04:27.056 --> 00:04:29.348
So this isn't a fair die.

75
00:04:30.016 --> 00:04:33.990
Still, these probabilities must add up to

76
00:04:34.070 --> 00:04:37.530
1, or 100%, because together

77
00:04:37.610 --> 00:04:42.212
all the possible outcomes cover every
possibility.

78
00:04:42.292 --> 00:04:45.352
Each probability lies between 0 and 1,

79
00:04:45.432 --> 00:04:48.572
and the probabilities of all these outcomes
must sum to 1.

80
00:04:49.024 --> 00:04:51.615
Now let's look at the complementary event.

81
00:04:51.695 --> 00:04:54.351
What does that mean?

83
00:04:54.431 --> 00:05:00.311
Here, event A is 'rolling a 4', with a
one-in-six chance.

84
00:05:00.391 --> 00:05:02.755
How can I calculate

85
00:05:02.835 --> 00:05:05.597
the probability of its complement?

86
00:05:05.677 --> 00:05:08.325
In other words, the probability of not rolling
a 4.

87
00:05:08.405 --> 00:05:10.940
In this simple example, I could

88
00:05:11.020 --> 00:05:15.396
add the other outcomes' probabilities: 1/12 +
1/4, and so on.

89
00:05:15.776 --> 00:05:18.674
But there's an easier way.

90
00:05:18.754 --> 00:05:22.200
'Rolling a 4' and its complement,

91
00:05:22.280 --> 00:05:25.604
'not rolling a 4', cover all the
possibilities.

92
00:05:25.684 --> 00:05:29.068
Since the total probability must be

93
00:05:29.148 --> 00:05:32.594
1, the probability of not rolling a 4

94
00:05:32.674 --> 00:05:36.260
is 1 minus the probability of rolling a 4.

95
00:05:36.352 --> 00:05:39.388
Here, the probability

96
00:05:39.468 --> 00:05:44.663
of event A, rolling a 4, is 1/6.

97
00:05:44.743 --> 00:05:47.919
So 1 minus 1/6 gives 5/6.

98
00:05:47.999 --> 00:05:51.114
The probability of not rolling a 4

99
00:05:51.194 --> 00:05:54.949
is therefore simply 5/6, exactly the same
value

100
00:05:55.029 --> 00:05:58.404
we'd get by adding all the other
probabilities.

101
00:05:58.484 --> 00:06:01.460
This is called the complement rule.

102
00:06:01.891 --> 00:06:03.872
Now we'll look at two more rules together,

103
00:06:03.952 --> 00:06:07.023
because comparing them helps answer this
question:

104
00:06:07.103 --> 00:06:09.093
when do we add probabilities,

105
00:06:09.173 --> 00:06:11.344
and when do we multiply them?

106
00:06:11.424 --> 00:06:14.045
This is a very common source of confusion.

107
00:06:14.125 --> 00:06:17.105
Imagine we're studying water quality.

108
00:06:17.185 --> 00:06:20.486
We classify it as very poor,

109
00:06:20.566 --> 00:06:23.487
poor, good or very good.

110
00:06:23.899 --> 00:06:28.523
This is an ordinal categorical variable,

111
00:06:28.603 --> 00:06:33.656
because the categories have a natural order.

112
00:06:33.736 --> 00:06:38.146
We'll call a site acceptable if its water
quality is

113
00:06:38.226 --> 00:06:43.136
good or very good. Here, there's a 25% chance

114
00:06:43.216 --> 00:06:48.063
of good quality and a 35% chance of very good
quality.

115
00:06:49.115 --> 00:06:50.954
So, by our definition, a site is

116
00:06:51.034 --> 00:06:53.776
acceptable if its water quality is either good
or very good.

117
00:06:53.856 --> 00:06:57.445
Here we add the probabilities, because a
selected

118
00:06:57.525 --> 00:07:01.283
site can't belong to both categories at once:

119
00:07:01.363 --> 00:07:05.122
its quality is either good or very good.

120
00:07:05.202 --> 00:07:08.847
The probability of one or the other is their
sum.

121
00:07:08.927 --> 00:07:12.991
That gives 0.60: a 60% chance of being
acceptable.

122
00:07:13.819 --> 00:07:17.436
Now imagine we look at two different sites.

123
00:07:17.516 --> 00:07:19.642
For the first site,

124
00:07:19.722 --> 00:07:23.063
there is still a 60% chance of being
acceptable.

125
00:07:23.143 --> 00:07:26.800
Now take a second site, B. Suppose knowing
whether A

126
00:07:26.880 --> 00:07:31.930
is acceptable doesn't change the chances for
B.

127
00:07:32.010 --> 00:07:34.531
B could be acceptable or unacceptable,

128
00:07:34.611 --> 00:07:37.311
independently of what happened at A.

129
00:07:37.723 --> 00:07:41.193
That's what this diagram shows:

130
00:07:41.273 --> 00:07:44.796
a 60% chance for site A and a 60% chance for
site B.

131
00:07:44.876 --> 00:07:47.616
Now we ask: 'What is the probability

132
00:07:47.696 --> 00:07:50.800
that both sites are acceptable?'

133
00:07:50.880 --> 00:07:53.150
Because the events are independent,

134
00:07:53.230 --> 00:07:56.022
meaning that knowing one outcome doesn't
change

135
00:07:56.102 --> 00:07:59.391
the probability of the other, we multiply the
probabilities.

136
00:07:59.483 --> 00:08:03.758
So 0.60 × 0.60 gives a 36% chance

137
00:08:03.838 --> 00:08:08.727
that both A and B are acceptable.

138
00:08:09.595 --> 00:08:13.809
Let's recap: when do we add, and when do we
multiply?

139
00:08:13.889 --> 00:08:16.884
If we ask about one event or the other,

140
00:08:16.964 --> 00:08:20.330
and the events are mutually exclusive, meaning
they cannot

141
00:08:20.410 --> 00:08:23.616
happen at the same time, we add their
probabilities.

142
00:08:23.696 --> 00:08:26.373
A site's quality can't be both good and very
good.

143
00:08:26.453 --> 00:08:30.111
So the probability of good or very good is the
sum.

144
00:08:30.459 --> 00:08:33.244
For independent events, on the other hand,

145
00:08:33.324 --> 00:08:36.767
knowing about one doesn't change the
probability of the other.

146
00:08:36.847 --> 00:08:39.914
For instance, knowing A is acceptable tells us
nothing about B.

147
00:08:39.994 --> 00:08:43.202
To find the probability of both, we multiply.

148
00:08:43.282 --> 00:08:45.973
Be careful with this common source of
confusion.

149
00:08:46.053 --> 00:08:47.523
Always check whether the events are

150
00:08:47.603 --> 00:08:50.233
mutually exclusive or independent.

151
00:08:50.727 --> 00:08:54.294
There are some common mistakes to avoid.

152
00:08:54.374 --> 00:08:58.321
For example, we can't simply add the
probability

153
00:08:58.401 --> 00:09:02.403
that a site is acceptable to the probability
that it is very good.

154
00:09:02.483 --> 00:09:05.016
That's because the acceptable

155
00:09:05.096 --> 00:09:07.683
category, which includes good and very good,

156
00:09:07.763 --> 00:09:11.274
already includes the very good category.

157
00:09:11.354 --> 00:09:14.191
So here, we cannot add

158
00:09:14.271 --> 00:09:18.118
the 0.6 probability of an acceptable site to
the probability

159
00:09:18.198 --> 00:09:21.821
of a very good site: the latter is already
included.

160
00:09:21.901 --> 00:09:25.099
Watch out for double counting.

161
00:09:25.415 --> 00:09:28.043
Also watch out for a common cause.

162
00:09:28.123 --> 00:09:31.987
We said that for sites A and B we could

163
00:09:32.067 --> 00:09:36.107
multiply the probabilities if the two events
were independent.

164
00:09:36.187 --> 00:09:38.050
But if we're looking at

165
00:09:38.130 --> 00:09:40.993
water contamination at A and B,

166
00:09:41.073 --> 00:09:44.541
the same source might have polluted both
sites.

167
00:09:44.621 --> 00:09:46.937
In that case, the events are not

168
00:09:47.017 --> 00:09:49.465
independent, so we can't simply multiply their
probabilities.

169
00:09:49.545 --> 00:09:52.703
Always check the assumptions before adding or
multiplying

170
00:09:52.783 --> 00:09:56.331
probabilities, to avoid these common mistakes.

171
00:09:59.015 --> 00:10:02.515
Now let's consider another situation.

172
00:10:02.595 --> 00:10:06.355
Suppose we're working with mangroves

173
00:10:06.435 --> 00:10:10.326
and have planted 100 young seedlings.

174
00:10:10.406 --> 00:10:14.687
Our model gives a 70% chance, or probability
0.7,

175
00:10:14.767 --> 00:10:17.669
that a seedling survives its first year.

176
00:10:17.749 --> 00:10:22.272
That's the theoretical value in our model.

177
00:10:22.352 --> 00:10:27.019
It isn't necessarily exactly what we'll
observe

178
00:10:27.111 --> 00:10:30.981
every time we carry out the experiment,
because

179
00:10:31.061 --> 00:10:35.709
there are random fluctuations, just as with
the die.

180
00:10:35.789 --> 00:10:40.270
Among these 100 seedlings, we might get 68
survivors or 72.

181
00:10:40.350 --> 00:10:44.442
Those fluctuations still occur,

182
00:10:44.522 --> 00:10:49.195
and 0.7 is simply the probability specified by
our model.

183
00:10:50.471 --> 00:10:53.178
Let's take one example.

184
00:10:53.258 --> 00:10:56.777
After a year, 64 of our 100 seedlings

185
00:10:56.857 --> 00:11:00.493
have survived: a survival rate of 64%.

186
00:11:00.573 --> 00:11:02.932
The question is:

187
00:11:03.012 --> 00:11:05.370
what does this tell us about the model?

188
00:11:05.450 --> 00:11:08.796
Suppose we didn't know the probability

189
00:11:08.876 --> 00:11:12.047
of 0.7 to begin with. Or perhaps we did,

190
00:11:12.127 --> 00:11:16.075
but wanted to check whether our model was
valid.

191
00:11:16.167 --> 00:11:18.430
We've observed

192
00:11:18.510 --> 00:11:21.017
64%, not 70%. What does that mean?

193
00:11:21.097 --> 00:11:22.677
Is our model wrong?

194
00:11:22.757 --> 00:11:24.141
Should we change it?

195
00:11:24.221 --> 00:11:27.460
Or could this simply be due to random
fluctuations?

196
00:11:27.540 --> 00:11:29.461
This brings us to the fundamental difference

197
00:11:29.541 --> 00:11:32.195
between probability and statistics.

198
00:11:32.275 --> 00:11:35.368
When we start with a known model and use it

199
00:11:35.448 --> 00:11:39.499
to explore possible outcomes, we're working
with probability.

200
00:11:39.591 --> 00:11:42.869
When we start with data and try to

201
00:11:42.949 --> 00:11:46.411
build a model or estimate the true

202
00:11:46.491 --> 00:11:50.093
probability of an event, we're doing
statistics.

203
00:11:50.589 --> 00:11:53.420
Of course, the two fields overlap enormously:

204
00:11:53.500 --> 00:11:56.636
probability and statistics are closely
connected.

205
00:11:56.716 --> 00:11:59.852
That's why we've taken this detour before
introducing statistical measures.

206
00:11:59.932 --> 00:12:02.329
But keep this key distinction in mind:

207
00:12:02.409 --> 00:12:04.979
do we start with a model to explore what's
possible,

208
00:12:05.059 --> 00:12:07.623
or with data to build a model?

209
00:12:08.097 --> 00:12:10.612
Now, given a set of values,

210
00:12:10.692 --> 00:12:13.789
our next question is: how are they
distributed?

211
00:12:13.869 --> 00:12:16.563
Here we have some concentration measurements.

212
00:12:16.643 --> 00:12:19.427
Each dot represents one measured
concentration.

213
00:12:19.507 --> 00:12:21.888
If we display them in order,

214
00:12:21.968 --> 00:12:24.035
from smallest to largest, we get

215
00:12:24.115 --> 00:12:26.809
something like this, which will lead us to
histograms.

216
00:12:26.889 --> 00:12:30.469
The horizontal axis shows concentration,
increasing from left to right.

217
00:12:30.753 --> 00:12:34.453
Some values are close together, there is some

218
00:12:34.533 --> 00:12:38.339
spread, and one value, 14, looks rather large.

219
00:12:38.419 --> 00:12:41.161
So the next question is:

220
00:12:41.241 --> 00:12:44.142
how can we examine the distribution

221
00:12:44.222 --> 00:12:47.203
of these data? We'll develop that idea
together.
